ingdom held a favorable view towards  socialism and in 2016 more people had a  favorable view of socialism and  capitalism most claim they would rather  live in a socialist country the terms  and frames of debate are changing pretty  radically here two weeks ago CNBC ran a  headline capitalism is in crisis and  last week the Financial Times which  perhaps for some considerable period of  time has been considered to be the  newspaper outlet that would be the most  free-market and capitalist actually ran  its entire episode its entire issue on  capitalism time for a reset we see  anti-capitalist protests everywhere  hitting the streets nearly all the time  on occasion I have considered organizing  a pro capitalist protest but I don't  like being alone and I'm not quite sure  who else would turn up but I'm under no  illusions whatsoever that the public  mood is highly skeptical about  free-market liberalism and activists and  the media and politics in general is  moving against it Michael Moore the  famous documentary filmmaker in America  has actually said capitalist capitalism  is an evil and you cannot regulate evil  this is not an understated sort of  critique this is a pretty ballsy  critique of capitalism I actually find  on the side of the argument in British  politics when I listening to or  discussing things with politicians who  you would expect to be advocates of  free-market capitalism  I guess members of parliament in the  Conservative Party tend to be more  sympathetic to free-market capitalist  idea  Byron margin politicians in other  parties but they do tend to be  apologists so once you have the Michael  Morel saying capitalism is an evil and  you cannot regulate evil oftentimes I'll  be listening to a member of parliament  who you might well consider to be an  apologist or a defender of free-market  capitalism and they will always begin  their speech with I am a free-market  sort of person but and will then give me  a large list of ways in which they 