igher  would be less free what's the state of  the legal system and property rights can  you get two recourse through the courts  of law or are they wholly corrupt if you  do the deeds to a property be that a  house or something else is it respected  under the legal system sound money is  the is the country operating in a way  that it isn't piling up debts or  printing money or photo copiers in the  basement  what's the freedom to trade  internationally is it easy to sell your  goods abroad or your services abroad or  to bring them in and what's the amount  of regulation and the less is better  from the point of view of measuring  freedom according to this index a  relatively low spending government  relatively light regulation a good court  system but one that focuses on property  rights and the government that doesn't  spend beyond its means so you can argue  a bit about their methodology but that's  what they're trying to measure and if  you take their lists and you look  through all 162 nations and you look at  the freest quartile the top 25% of  countries typically towards the low tax  low regulation defend property rights  make sure you're operating sound money  sort of end well that freest quartile  has on average and income six times  higher than the least free quartile if  you're in that top 25% of nations which  indeed the United Kingdom is  the average individual income across  them is a little shy of 37 thousand US  dollars per annum and your ofin you're  in the bottom quartile the worst 25%  then the average income there is about  $6,000 per annum quite a difference and  even if you're worried about inequality  in your own country the bottom 10  percent of income earners in those top  quarter of countries make seven times  more than the bottom 10 percent of  earners in those lows in those lowest  least free countries so if you're in the  bottom end of a free country the bottom  10 percent your average earnings are a  little over ten thousand US dollars 